\begin{align*} F &= m\frac{v^2}{r} \end{align*}
Equating the two expressions for the force, we have
\begin{align*} m\frac{v^2}{r} &= -\frac{GMm}{r^2} \\ v^2 &= -\frac{GM}{r} \end{align*}
Since $v$ is a velocity, it must be positive. Thus, we have
\begin{align*} v^2 &= \frac{GM}{r} \\ v &= \sqrt{\frac{GM}{r}} \end{align*}
The mass of the Galaxy is given by $M = \frac{4}{3}\pi r_0^3 \rho_0$, where $r_0$ is the radius of the dark-matter halo and $\rho_0$ is the density of the dark-matter halo. Substituting this into the expression for $v$, we get
\begin{align*} v &= \sqrt{\frac{G \left(\frac{4}{3}\pi r_0^3 \rho_0\right)}{r}} \\ v &= \sqrt{\frac{4}{3} \pi G r_0^3 \rho_0 \frac{1}{r}} \\ v &= \sqrt{\frac{4}{3} \pi G r_0^3 \rho_0} \frac{1}{\sqrt{r}} \end{align*}
Thus, the rotation curve of the Galaxy is given by
\begin{align*} v(r) &= \sqrt{\frac{4}{3} \pi G r_0^3 \rho_0} \frac{1}{\sqrt{r}} \end{align*}
This is the rotation curve of the Galaxy at large distances.

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{\sqrt{\frac{4}{3} \pi G r_0^3 \rho_0} \frac{1}{\sqrt{r}}}$

