The marginal product of labor (MPL) is given by the partial derivative of $F(K, L)$ with respect to $L$:
\begin{align*} \label{eq:1} \frac{\partial F}{\partial L} &= \frac{1}{4} (K-1)^{-\frac{3}{4}} L^{-\frac{3}{4}} \end{align*}
The marginal product of capital (MPK) is given by the partial derivative of $F(K, L)$ with respect to $K$:
\begin{align*} \frac{\partial F}{\partial K} &= \frac{1}{4} (K-1)^{-\frac{3}{4}} L^{\frac{1}{4}} \end{align*}
The marginal rate of technical substitution (MRTS) of labor for capital is given by the ratio of the marginal product of labor to the marginal product of capital:
\begin{align*} \text{MRTS}_{L/K} &= \frac{\frac{\partial F}{\partial L}}{\frac{\partial F}{\partial K}} \\ &= \frac{\frac{1}{4} (K-1)^{-\frac{3}{4}} L^{-\frac{3}{4}}}{\frac{1}{4} (K-1)^{-\frac{3}{4}} L^{\frac{1}{4}}} \\ &= \frac{L^{-\frac{3}{4}}}{L^{\frac{1}{4}}} \\ &= L^{-\frac{3}{4} + \frac{1}{4}} \\ &= L^{-\frac{2}{4}} \\ &= L^{-\frac{1}{2}} \\ &= \frac{1}{\sqrt{L}} \end{align*}
Thus, the marginal rate of technical substitution of labor for capital is $\frac{1}{\sqrt{L}}$.

